Galant "Circle Progression" phrase plan (Bars 1-8)
Progression: I-V-vi-iii-IV-I-ii(7)-V(-I), mapped to the merged library's
standard chord names (C as tonic reference): C-G-Am-Em-F-C-Dm-G(-C)

Goal: choose which bar-variant (motif) to use at each position so that (a)
the melodic contour (rising/falling/neither, major-3rd rule) reinforces the
harmonic function at that point in the phrase, and (b) each bar's first note
lands close to the previous bar's last note (common tone or step), so the
melody reads as one continuous line rather than 8 disconnected fragments.

Per-chord data available (merged_bars.json, treble voice, n = usable bars):
  C  (n=60): rising=18 falling=19 neither=23 - first notes mostly C/G/E, last mostly C/E/G
  G  (n=38): rising=17 falling=5  neither=16 - first notes mostly G/D/B, last mostly G/B/D
  Am (n=36): rising=16 falling=3  neither=17 - first notes mostly A/E/C, last mostly A/C/E
  Em (n=34): rising=16 falling=3  neither=15 - first notes mostly E/B/G, last mostly E/G/B
  F  (n=37): rising=16 falling=5  neither=16 - first notes mostly F/C/A, last mostly F/A/C
  Dm (n=35): rising=16 falling=3  neither=16 - first notes mostly D/A/F, last mostly D/F/A

Caveat: every chord except C has a small "falling" pool (only 3-5 bars). C is
falling-rich (19) because most of "Descending motifs" landed on C. Save true
"falling" contour for the bars where it matters harmonically (the two
strongest cadential drops: bar 6 back to tonic, bar 8's dominant close) and
use "neither" elsewhere rather than forcing falling where the pool is thin.

Recommended contour per bar, and why:
  Bar 1  C   neither  - calm opening statement, establishes the tonic before motion begins
  Bar 2  G   rising   - root motion C->G is the phrase's first lift; rising reinforces it
  Bar 3  Am  rising   - continues the upward drift (G->A is a step up)
  Bar 4  Em  neither  - mediant "turn"/suspension point before the second half; let it hover
  Bar 5  F   rising   - fresh subdominant energy opens the consequent phrase
  Bar 6  C   falling  - F->C is a strong resolution; falling melody mirrors the harmonic drop
  Bar 7  Dm  rising   - pre-dominant, building toward the final cadence
  Bar 8  G   falling  - dominant close; falling contour mirrors the melody settling toward the
                         implied tonic (bar 9/next phrase)

Suggested connective tones between bars (root-position triads, closest voice leading):
  C->G:  common tone G                (C-E-G share G with G-B-D)
  G->Am: step (B->C or D->C)          (leading tone / step into Am's 3rd)
  Am->Em: common tone E               (A-C-E share E with E-G-B)
  Em->F: half step (E->F or B->C)     (classic iii->IV melodic motion)
  F->C:  common tone C                (F-A-C share C with C-E-G)
  C->Dm: step (C->D or E->D)          (into ii)
  Dm->G: common tone D                (D-F-A share D with G-B-D)
  G->C:  half step (B->C, leading tone resolution) - the authentic-cadence close

Worked example (greedy: match each bar's first note to the previous bar's last
note within the required contour, using real bars already in the library):

  bar  chord  contour   measure  start  end   gap-to-prev-end (semitones)
  1    C      neither   1        C      C     -
  2    G      rising    77       B      E     1
  3    Am     rising    148      E      A     0
  4    Em     neither   189      G      G     2
  5    F      rising    38       F      A#    2
  6    C      falling   13       C      F     2
  7    Dm     rising    112      F      A#    0
  8    G      falling   85       B      D     1

All gaps are 2 semitones or less (mostly common tone or a single step), so
this specific set of 8 bars can be built in the app right now (add C, G, Am,
Em, F, C, Dm, G to the pattern, then pick bar 1, 77, 148, 189, 38, 13, 112, 85
in the variant picker for each slot) and should read as one coherent,
directionally-sensible phrase rather than 8 unrelated fragments.

This is one valid solution, not the only one - any bar whose start is within
a step or two of the previous bar's end, and whose own contour matches the
table above, will work about as well. Bars picked at random from a chord's
full pool (ignoring contour/endpoint) will sometimes still connect smoothly
(most bars start/end on chord tones) but will not reliably reinforce the
phrase's rise-fall shape the way this planned selection does.
